Byron A Jeff wrote:
> >#1: Don't forget about prayer!
>
> >This is perhaps the most important thing.  Prayer can get you out of an
> >absurd number of problems, too many really.
>
> But it comes at a cost. You can't pray too often. You have to not piss
> your god off. You can to get in really bad straights to be helped out.

You also have to keep your luck positive (meaning that prayer fails
fairly frequently in Sokoban if you don't play it perfectly).  But so
long as you do that and don't abuse it, prayer usually works.  My "too
many really" comment comes from observing that a feature like that in
Rogue would have wrecked that game.


> I don't really start praying until I get a coaligned altar. Then frankly
> I abuse the altar for all it's worth.

I'd go ahead and pray anyway if I was about to die.  (Which is what
prayer for getting out of trouble is meant for anyway.)


> >#2: Keep moving!
>
> >The longer you're in the game without good equipment, the more chance
> >the game will send in something nasty you can't handle, or some
> >fortuitous combination of other foes.
>
> Not sure about the advise. Do you mean run away?!

I mean to not hang out too long.  It's more important to explore new
levels, with the increased chance of finding useful equipment, than to
stay put and find that last secret door, for example.  This is a word
of advice that I'd do well to follow myself, by the way, I usually
clean out each level before I go to the next.  Running away in a pinch,
well, that's often good advice but I usually figure it's obvious.


> You can price ID lamps in a shop. Both oil and magic lamps are ridculously
> easy to figure out and usually there's a shop or three high in the dungeon.

That's another solution, although I find oil lamps aren't valuable
enough to attempt to conserve in this way.


> Holy water is the tougher find in order to bless. The ways to get holy
> water early in the game are dangerous for early characters.

This is true.  Still, randomly-found water isn't hard to find, there's
a 1/3 chance of a co-aligned altar in minetown, and for non-temple
altars it's not hard to convert an altar if you've been doing any
amount of hostile monster killing.


> There was somewhat of a debate going on in another recent thread. I was
> advocating about the same strategy you were outlining above. Another poster
> (may have been John) pointed out that reflection is really important in
> the early game because nasty ray wands such as sleep, cold, fire, lighting,
> and yes even death can appear early in the game.

Magic protection covers death wands.


> He also pointed out that
> unless you're chaotic, there are Artifacts that give good effects and
> confer magic resistance. So SDSM may be a better choice in the early game
> even though there's a even chance of getting an amulet of reflection
> early on in Sokoban.

Hmmm... this is interesting.  Although I observe that whichever one you
get, it's better to get one that you'll want to keep through the end
instead of one you'll discard, so it still remains that one is not
always more desirable than the other.  (Also, reflection doesn't
protect against polymorph traps, which are sometimes found in the
mines.)


> So unless you actually have reflection, it's the more critical intrinsic
> early game. Even as you start to need magic resistance with poly and
> level teleport traps, the effects are not too terrible. In fact if you
> have SDSM on a poly trap turns you into the dragon.

If you are unlucky enough to get polymorphed into something large when
already polymorphed into a dragon courtesy of the DSM, it'll destroy
the armor (a fact I discovered way back in my first ascension).
Overall however I agree poly traps are not a huge consideration for
choosing SDSM, although you could lose cloaks because of it.


> >[...]In the
> >early game, where every point of AC is important and you don't have so
> >much stuff that getting weighed down by armor is a problem, then put on
> >the best you can find, but once you have mithril drop all the rest.
>
> Unless you're a spellcaster, where mithril makes you virtually useless.

Agreed.  For spellcasters, leather or studded leather are better
choices.


> The mines are tough to handle because they have so much open space.
> You have to learn to duck and run while you let your buff pet take
> care of things in the upper mines. Again a pacifist strategy early
> can be helpful.

Hm, I agree with this.  Usually when I'm in those open situations in
the mines, I find a bottleneck.  I should have made that #11 in fact,
for when I think back to the thing that changed when I started finding
Nethack to be not as hard as the reputation attached to the game, the
thing, besides lots of spoiler reading, was making better use of
doorways and corridors to cut down the number of foes striking per
turn.


> While in pre protection mode, I literally lead them out of the shop and
> let them re-mimic outside the shop. You almost never have to melee them
> unless you just want to.

That's helpful if you can get the mimic all the way through the
corridor.  Sometimes shops are at the end of a long path and it's a
pain to do it that way.

